## Problem Statement

Given an array of integers `nums`, sort the array in ascending order and return it.

**LeetCode Question No.:** 912 — Sort an Array

## Approach

Use **Merge Sort**.

1. Find the middle index of the current range.
2. Recursively sort the left half.
3. Recursively sort the right half.
4. Merge the two sorted halves using a temporary vector.
5. Copy the merged elements back into the original array.

The recursion stops when `start >= end`, because a subarray with zero or one element is already sorted.

## Time Complexity

`O(n log n)`

* The array is divided into `log n` levels.
* Each level takes `O(n)` time for merging.

## Space Complexity

`O(n)` for the temporary vector used during merging, plus `O(log n)` recursion stack.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> sortArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1);
        return nums;
    }

    void sort(vector<int>& nums, int start, int end) {
        if (start < end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;

            sort(nums, start, mid);
            sort(nums, mid + 1, end);

            merge(nums, start, mid, end);
        }
    }

    void merge(vector<int>& nums, int start, int mid, int end) {
        vector<int> temp;

        int i = start;
        int j = mid + 1;

        while (i <= mid && j <= end) {
            if (nums[i] <= nums[j]) {
                temp.push_back(nums[i]);
                i++;
            } else {
                temp.push_back(nums[j]);
                j++;
            }
        }

        while (i <= mid) {
            temp.push_back(nums[i]);
            i++;
        }

        while (j <= end) {
            temp.push_back(nums[j]);
            j++;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < temp.size(); i++) {
            nums[i + start] = temp[i];
        }
    }
};
```

